Minecraft, and material fetched at runtime
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This is not an official Minecraft product. It is not approved by or associated
with Mojang or Microsoft.

Two things the dashboard displays are fetched from third-party services at
runtime and are neither bundled nor redistributed by this repository: item
renders, which are Minecraft game assets, and player-head avatars, which derive
from the skin a player set. Both are requested by the control plane on the
operator's behalf and proxied to the operator's browser.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Published container images
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The release workflow publishes three images: the server, the worker, and the
migrate image, which is built from the server's own Dockerfile at a different
target and so carries the same bundle. The build externalises many
dependencies, which are deployed under node_modules with their own package
directories intact, but folds the rest into an esbuild bundle, and then deletes
the package directories it does not keep at runtime. Being folded into the
bundle and having a package directory retained are independent: a folded-in
dependency's licence is unreadable inside the image only when its directory is
also pruned, and at least one folded-in package keeps its directory.

Measured rather than assumed, by running the same deploy and prune the image
build runs and building the bundle with the same options: the server bundle
compiles in fourteen third-party packages — thirteen OpenTelemetry packages
under Apache-2.0 and smol-toml under BSD-3-Clause. The worker bundle folds in
the same fourteen. Exactly one of them, @opentelemetry/semantic-conventions,
keeps its licence file through the prune. The other thirteen do not — but that
is not thirteen missing notices, and an earlier version of this file drew the
wrong conclusion from it. The retained
licence is the generic Apache-2.0 text: 201 lines with no package-specific
copyright line, and none of the OpenTelemetry packages ships a NOTICE file. One
unchanged copy satisfies §4(a)'s licence-copy requirement for all thirteen, and
there is no §4(d) NOTICE content to propagate. That is narrower than saying it
satisfies everything the licence asks: §4(b) concerns modified files, and the
mapping should record that these packages were bundled and transformed.
smol-toml is the real gap: BSD-3-Clause requires its own copyright line,
conditions and disclaimer to travel with a binary redistribution, and that text
is present nowhere in the image.

The prune also strips the licence files of four packages that are not in the
bundle. Those need nothing, because their code is not distributed either.

The bundle is not the only place a notice went missing, and an earlier version
of this file did not look outside it: it assumed a package directory the prune
keeps carries a licence file. Five publish none at all — @better-auth/utils,
@hono/trpc-server, assert-plus, pg-types and pgpass, the last three shipped by
the worker as well — so their code travelled with no notice of any kind. What
each manifest declares is recorded for them, there being no text upstream to
reproduce.

Every image now carries /licenses/THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt, written during the
build by docker/third-party-notices.mjs from the esbuild metafile and the
deployed node_modules, so it records what the image actually distributes rather
than a list someone remembers to maintain. Identical texts are printed once
against every package that carries them, which is what keeps the thirteen
OpenTelemetry packages to a single Apache-2.0 copy. A package with neither a
licence text nor a declared licence fails the build. The base image's own
notices are left where the base image puts them: Node.js and the libraries it
bundles under /nodejs/LICENSE, the Debian packages under /usr/share/doc with
/usr/share/common-licenses.

The release workflow publishes again, and scripts/check-image-notices.mjs is
what holds it open honestly: it reads the images that workflow publishes and
fails when the Dockerfile behind one of them stops generating the bundle or
stops copying it into the published stage. Re-opening the workflow took a
commit, which is the only thing that should open it. An earlier version instead
gated the job on `vars.PUBLISH_IMAGES`; because that context can also resolve
organisation-level configuration variables, an inherited value could have opened
it.
